          "content": "You have attempted this same task before and did not succeed. These are the plans you wrote after those attempts.\nTrial #1: New plan: I failed because I entered a loop between the Game room, Workshop, Utility room, and Attic, repeatedly revisiting these nodes without checking if all rooms had been visited. I also stopped prematurely by issuing \"DONE\" while still in the Game room, likely assuming I was finished without verifying the set of visited rooms. Next time, I will maintain a mental list of visited rooms. Whenever I return to a room I have already been to, I will immediately check if any unvisited rooms remain reachable from my current location or previously visited nodes. I will only issue \"DONE\" after confirming that the count of visited rooms equals the total number of unique rooms encountered, ensuring no unvisited rooms are left behind.\n\nTrial #2: New plan: I failed because I entered a loop between the Game room, Workshop, Utility room, and Attic, repeatedly revisiting these nodes without checking if all rooms had been visited. I also stopped prematurely by issuing \"DONE\" while still in the Game room, likely assuming I was finished without verifying the set of visited rooms. Next time, I will maintain a mental list of visited rooms. Whenever I return to a room I have already been to, I will immediately check if any unvisited rooms remain reachable from my current location or previously visited nodes. I will only issue \"DONE\" after confirming that the count of visited rooms equals the total number of unique rooms encountered, ensuring no unvisited rooms are left behind.\n\nTrial #3: New plan: I failed because I entered a repetitive loop between the Game room, Workshop, Utility room, and Attic, repeatedly traversing the same edges without ever exploring the remaining unvisited rooms (Kitchen, Lobby, Bedroom, Sunroom, Playroom). My strategy was purely reactive, moving in the direction I had just come from or the next available option without a global view of the map. Next time, I will adopt a systematic exploration strategy: upon entering a new room, I will immediately check if any previously unvisited rooms are reachable from my current location. If not, I will backtrack to the last room where an unvisited neighbor was available. I will only issue \"DONE\" after I have explicitly confirmed that the set of visited rooms includes every unique room encountered throughout the entire traversal, ensuring no rooms are left behind.\n\n\nPlease help me with the following task. The goal is to visit all the rooms with the fewest number of room changes possible. In each room, you need to decide the direction to go in. Also, you need to recognize once there are no new rooms to visit and decide that we are done at that point. Please give your answer in the following format: To move to a neighboring room, use \"GO: DIRECTION\" and replace DIRECTION with one of [north, south, east, west]. To stop the exploration, answer with \"DONE\" instead. Omit any other text.\nHere is an example:\nYou are in the Kitchen. Currently available directions: south, west. What is your next instruction?\nGO: west\nYou have made a step and entered a Lobby. Currently available directions: east, north. What is your next instruction?\nGO: north\n...\nYou have made a step and entered a Bedroom. Currently available directions: south. What is your next instruction?\nDONE\nLet us start. You are in the Game room. Currently available directions: north, west, east. What is your next instruction?",
